codec_lzma: fix inverted MIN/MAX in the dictionary-size clamp

lzma_compute_aligned_dictionary_size() reimplements the LZMA SDK's
LzmaEncProps_Normalize() so the decoder can size its dictionary the way
the encoder did, without linking the encoder. The reduceSize clamp is
transcribed with BOTH operators inverted, so it never reduces anything:

    const uint32_t max = MIN(kReduceMin, reduceSize);  /* = 4096 */
    dictSize = MAX(max, dictSize);                     /* = dictSize, always */

The SDK it reproduces (C/LzmaEnc.c, LzmaEncProps_Normalize) does the
opposite -- floor reduceSize at kReduceMin, then take the SMALLER of that
and dictSize:

    UInt32 v = (UInt32)p->reduceSize;
    const UInt32 kReduceMin = ((UInt32)1 << 12);
    if (v < kReduceMin) v = kReduceMin;      /* v = MAX(reduceSize, 4096) */
    if (p->dictSize > v) p->dictSize = v;    /* dictSize = MIN(dictSize, v) */

Effect: dictSize keeps level 9's default of 1 << 26 = 64 MiB instead of
dropping to reduceSize. For CD-LZ, cdlz_codec_init() passes
(hunkbytes / CD_FRAME_SIZE) * CD_MAX_SECTOR_DATA = 18,816 bytes, so
LzmaDec_Allocate reserves 64 MiB to decode 18 KB -- a 2,730x
over-allocation, once per chd_file handle.

Nothing is wrong with the decode path itself: codec init runs once per
chd_open (libchdr_chd.c:1089) and lzma_codec_decompress only calls
LzmaDec_Init (a state reset) then LzmaDec_DecodeToBuf, so there is no
per-hunk allocation. The buffer is LZMA's sliding-window history, which
doubles as the decode output ring; dicBufSize bounds how far back a match
may reference. CHD compresses each hunk independently and LzmaDec_Init
rewinds dicPos per hunk, so a match can never reach past one hunk. The
remaining 99.97% of the 64 MiB is never read or written.

That is why this has gone unnoticed: untouched pages are not resident, so
the cost is address space, not RAM -- for an ordinary process. It becomes
real RAM for a consumer that calls mlockall(), which a real-time CD/DA
player must, since locked pages are populated on allocation.

Measured on a DE10-Nano (ARM32, 488 MB RAM) decoding a Sonic CD .chd,
one process, no mlockall until the last line:

    start             VmSize=  2,816  VmRSS=  1,456 kB
    2 handles open    VmSize=134,800  VmRSS=  2,536 kB
    after 64 decodes  VmSize=134,800  VmRSS=  2,580 kB
    after mlockall    VmSize=134,800  VmRSS=134,800 kB

An LD_PRELOAD malloc interposer shows the two offending allocations
directly: malloc(67108932) x2 from lzma_fast_alloc, and 67108932 - 68 is
exactly 64 MiB. With this patch neither appears. A real consumer that
opens two handles (one for a prefetch thread) and locks its memory drops
from VmRSS 155,972 kB to 27,996 kB -- about 128 MB, 82%, reclaimed.

Correctness is unaffected: the stream was encoded with the reduced
dictionary, so the decoder never needed more than one hunk of history.
Verified byte-exact rather than merely error-free -- FNV-1a over all
31,984 decoded hunks of the test image is ab0ea6b040de538a before and
after, with 0 read failures.

Upstream status: bug in upstream master (6cde534) and present at the
v0.3.0 tag; candidate for upstreaming, not yet sent.

diff --git a/src/libchdr_codec_lzma.c b/src/libchdr_codec_lzma.c
--- a/src/libchdr_codec_lzma.c
+++ b/src/libchdr_codec_lzma.c
@@ -164,9 +164,9 @@
 	if (dictSize > reduceSize)
 	{
 		const uint32_t kReduceMin = (uint32_t)1 << 12;
-		const uint32_t max = MIN(kReduceMin, reduceSize);
+		const uint32_t v = MAX(kReduceMin, reduceSize);
 
-		dictSize = MAX(max, dictSize);
+		dictSize = MIN(v, dictSize);
 	}
 
 	/* LzmaEnc_SetProps */
